   178	This image is build- and pack-verified with the corrected `tf_test info` and
   179	the first speaker-playback implementation. SDMC1 block I/O, FATFS mounting,
   180	and bounded file read/write are board-verified. The preceding speaker image
   181	entered playback without sound and blocked after starting the polling path.
   182	The first diagnosis incorrectly focused on the FIFO wait itself. The actual
   183	permanent block was synchronous buffer completion filling the nxplayer message
   184	queue before the same play thread could receive from it. A subsequent
   185	one-buffer-per-enqueue workaround avoided the full queue but stranded the
   186	already-pending FINAL buffer and still hung at the end of tone or WAV playback.
   187	This image restores the previously audible FIFO setup and moves CPU polling to
   188	a dedicated low-priority kernel thread that continuously drains pending data.
   189	It awaits board retest. Speaker playback remains the only newly introduced,
   190	unverified peripheral.
   191	
   192	## 4. Runtime Architecture
   193	
   194	### 4.1 Memory and Image Layout
   195	
   405	
   406	The first board run reached tone playback but stopped returning to NSH. An
   407	initial patch changed FIFO flush and mono/stereo setup, after which the
   408	previously audible WAV path also became silent. Comparison with the supplier
   409	baseline showed that initialization intentionally enables both TX FIFO
   410	channels for this DSPK1 path, so the current image restores that known-audible
   411	register behavior.
   412	
   413	The permanent wait was above the FIFO polling loop. CPU mode completed buffers
   414	synchronously inside `AUDIOIOC_START`, and each completion sent a message to
   415	nxplayer. A one-second stereo tone requires about 24 buffers, while the queue
   416	holds only `CONFIG_AUDIO_NUM_BUFFERS + 8`, currently 10 messages. The sender
   417	and receiver were the same play thread, so the full queue caused a permanent
   418	self-wait before `AUDIOIOC_START` could return. The first attempted correction
   419	processed one buffer per enqueue call, but that is also invalid: when nxplayer
   420	marks a buffer FINAL, it can remain behind another pending buffer, and no later
   421	enqueue exists to trigger it. This exactly explains tone hanging and WAV
   422	stopping in its later portion.
   423	
   424	CPU polling now runs in a persistent kernel thread at priority 90, below NSH.
   425	`AUDIOIOC_START` and enqueue only update the queue and wake the thread; the
   426	thread processes one buffer at a time, rechecks stop state between buffers, and
   427	continues until pending is empty or FINAL completes. Queue insertion and XRUN
   428	wakeup are updated in the same critical section to avoid a lost wake at the
   429	empty-queue boundary. Stop waits for the current buffer through a dedicated
   430	mutex before aborting or deinitializing AUDIO. The 2000 ms FIFO timeout remains
   431	as a hardware-stall guard, and errors return queued buffers plus COMPLETE.
